 "requested_at": "2026-11-12T19:52:27.482173Z",
 "text": "The clause is most likely **not enforceable**. Section 106 of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (S.O. 2006, c. 17) generally prohibits a landlord from collecting or requiring any security deposit or similar payment beyond what the Act permits, which is at most the first rental period's rent as a rent deposit. A $1,000 payment held as security for all of the tenant's obligations, and returnable only when the tenancy ends, falls outside that permitted category. Calling the payer a \"guarantor\" does not change the analysis, because the payment is still a deposit the landlord collects in connection with the tenancy.\n\nTwo related points:\n\n- **Section 4** (no contracting out) would void any term that purports to waive or limit the tenant's rights under the Act, so the clause would not be saved by being agreed to.\n- **The result depends on the rent.** If the rent is such that $1,000 is within what the Act allows as a rent deposit, the analysis changes. Here the amount is treated as an impermissible security deposit.\n\nI'm working from memory and have not checked the current consolidated text, so confirm the wording of s. 106 (and the rent-deposit limit in the preceding sections) on e-Laws before relying on this.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 106",
